- Okay.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:48.390
- Unca, U N C A, uncas.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:49.893
- Uncles. Uncles.
- Okay.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.300
- There were quite a few men in in my life

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.490
in my family who protected me.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:57.390
- Yeah.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:59.910
- And they were old school.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:00.743
- Yeah.

00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:03.420
- Farmers. They wanted the
girls to be self-sufficient.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.780
Drive a car, go fly
fishing, tie the flies.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.100
- Yeah.
- Bring the boat in.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.733
And he and all my uncles would say,

00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:14.070
"Baby, I want you and the girls

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:15.570
to do everything the boys can do

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.120
so we don't have to worry all the time."

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:18.953
Give them a break.

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:21.330
When they came back from
Vietnam, from the war,

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:25.590
there's nothing harder than
they don't have arms and legs.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.210
But we went fishing.

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.800
We took care of the cars.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.510
We filled the cars up with gas.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.550
- Yeah.
- Firewood was chopped.

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.820
My family's from the Black
West, Wyoming and Colorado.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.631
And so we, Wyoming.

00:01:37.631 --> 00:01:38.470
- I ain't never heard of that.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.621
- Yeah. I gave that
information to Richard Pryor.

00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:41.943
- Black people from Wyoming?

00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:44.040
- Hey, you saw Blazing Saddles.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:44.873
- Blazing Saddles.

00:01:44.873 --> 00:01:46.890
- I gave that information to Richard Pryor

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.690
when he was co-writing with Mel Brooks.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.130
- Are you serious right now?

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.120
- Yes, ma'am.
- Very serious.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:52.800
- I want you to tell that story

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.000
because I think this is amazing.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.470
I learned about, because I,

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.180
equine therapy is so important

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.250
and I've had friends
really benefit from it.

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.110
And you did as well as a young kid, right?

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:02.640
- Yes.
- Can you tell that story?

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.140
I love this story.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.940
I don't love how it started, obviously.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.710
But I just mean like, you ended up,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:08.970
was it with your grandfather?

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.970
- My grandfather and my grandmother
had the farm in Wyoming.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.030
I was traumatized.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:18.873
I had a couple of incidences
that were tragedy,

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:20.813
very traumatic.

00:02:20.813 --> 00:02:24.534
And my grandfather took me up to the farm

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:27.720
where there's animals, large animals.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.340
And because of the trauma
and I still stutter,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.963
I couldn't speak.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.510
And daddy ran.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:41.278
My uncle went to the juke
joint and left me in the car.

00:02:41.278 --> 00:02:42.870
"Pammy, don't get on the horse.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:43.800
Don't get near the horse.

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:45.335
Don't get near the bull."

00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:47.280
What did Pammy do?

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.630
Soon as they turned their
back and walked away,

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.750
there was this gigantic
draft horse in the pasture.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.775
And I walked over to
him and I looked at him

00:02:57.775 --> 00:03:00.600
and something started happening to me

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:05.600
where I now, this wall of
fear and pain was dissipating.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.880
And I climbed the fence

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.616
and I got on top of him and I stroked him.

00:03:11.616 --> 00:03:16.500
And I remember he looked
at me with the big eyes

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.360
and I could feel his heart beating and

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.390
- That connection.
- I pulled up on top of him.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.820
My little legs were
splayed out and my braids.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.130
And he says, "I'm gonna
take you for a little walk."

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.110
And every step he made,

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.090
you could feel the ground shake.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:30.923
- Power.

00:03:30.923 --> 00:03:32.490
- And it came up through
his legs, through my body.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.810
And I could see a horizon
that was totally different.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.600
No rooftops, no fear, no one coming at me,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.790
no one choking me, strangling me,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:43.383
fighting me as a little girl.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.770
And he took me for a ride

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.653
and all of a sudden I could breathe again.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.610
- It's amazing that connection
with animals. It is.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:54.523
And clap. That's amazing.

00:03:54.523 --> 00:03:55.623
- I started talking.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.720
- And that's what I find amazing.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:03:59.550
Your stutter started to dissipate.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.070
- It began, it left.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:02.940
- It's powerful.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.353
That kind of therapy is powerful.

00:04:05.353 --> 00:04:07.830
So we are honoring Dr. King today.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:10.620
So Pam, when is the first
time you learned about him?

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.990
- When my mother fell to her knees.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:13.823
- Wow.

00:04:13.823 --> 00:04:16.757
- Watching him march with other

00:04:16.757 --> 00:04:18.900
- That's a powerful memory.
- Yes.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.523
With other men who were knowing
they're gonna take the hit.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.903
And the women who had to take the hit.

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.860
Barbara Jordan, Shirley Chisholm,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.293
Gloria Steinem, Bella Abzug.

00:04:36.456 --> 00:04:39.240
We had one little black and
white television in our den.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:42.280
And my mom was watching them march

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:45.600
in front of, in Washington.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.250
She fell to her knees.

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:49.320
- That's powerful for kids.
- And she started crying.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:50.640
- Yeah.

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.890
- And she said they
just, and then she said

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.780
but the whites who love
us can't fight for us

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.710
because they're considered lovers.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.960
And they will have
their house burned down,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.410
or their church burned down,
or they could be lynched.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.320
And my mom, she was inconsolable

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.847
and she said you saw all the people

00:05:11.847 --> 00:05:15.153
and the faces and the
hearts beyond the heart.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.142
- Sometimes it's hard to see
the hope through the hate.

00:05:19.142 --> 00:05:22.110
There's so much hate that is
involved and so much evil.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:22.943
I feel like that is a

00:05:22.943 --> 00:05:23.776
- It's a mental illness.

00:05:23.776 --> 00:05:25.050
You know, hate is a mental illness.

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:26.910
- 100%.
- It's not normal.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:29.199
- That's a huge problem in our country.

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:32.040
But it's also hard to find hope.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.210
That little light.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:36.065
- I find, I search all the time.

00:05:36.065 --> 00:05:36.960
- I wanna say this to those.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.990
So Cedric, you played Dr.
King's closest friend,

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:40.823
Ralph Abernathy.

00:05:40.823 --> 00:05:41.656
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:05:41.656 --> 00:05:43.320
And in a Spike Lee movie.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.600
So did you learn anything about Dr. King

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.843
that surprised you that maybe wasn't ever,

00:05:47.843 --> 00:05:49.634
isn't showcased on the regular?

00:05:49.634 --> 00:05:51.180
- Well, in this movie Son of the South,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:52.744
I actually had studied to do,

00:05:52.744 --> 00:05:57.423
I've been cast for Ralph
Abernathy in Selma, too.

00:05:59.692 --> 00:06:02.010
But what I found out the
most is their sense of humor.

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.680
What I really loved is
between the two of them,

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.520
they were really friends and
they had this buddy energy

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.780
that I think that you miss

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:14.460
when someone is such a
great historic figure

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.260
is that you always see them as serious.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:17.640
And what I really loved is

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.440
that they were friends and buddies

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.870
and had this shorthand with
each other and laughed a lot.

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:22.703
- Yeah.

00:06:22.703 --> 00:06:26.130
- And so therefore, it was
that understanding that,

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.942
how do you do a work like that

00:06:27.942 --> 00:06:30.840
when every day seems so serious?

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.330
You have to have some degree of

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.240
where life is comfortable
and you and that,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:37.260
that was his partner.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.093
And I like that.

00:06:38.093 --> 00:06:41.130
I love the idea of that.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:41.963
- Well, it's interesting to me.

00:06:41.963 --> 00:06:43.560
- They had each other for that.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.550
- It's interesting to me, too, though.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.470
I find like the most deep people I know

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:47.730
are usually comedians.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:48.990
Even if it's not their job.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:49.823
They're just
- Yeah.

00:06:49.823 --> 00:06:51.780
- They're the people that
make you laugh so hard.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.880
And they have such a deep understanding,

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:55.380
a deep intelligence for a lot of things.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.213
And it is,

00:06:56.213 --> 00:06:58.620
they are the more serious
people in my life, as well.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:06:59.453
- Sure.

00:06:59.453 --> 00:07:00.390
- But I think you have to find that humor

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.280
to have that, if balance existed,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.050
but to have that kind of balance, too.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:05.280
Because you wanna address things

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.260
and you wanna be a part of things,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:08.760
but at the same time
it can be overwhelming

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.800
and it feels so big that
you can't conquer it.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:11.633
You know?

00:07:11.633 --> 00:07:12.466
So you need humor.

00:07:12.466 --> 00:07:15.480
I wanna ask you, though,
about The Plot Thickens.

